a variety of the race ''Triticum turgidum.''—'''Saracen’s wheat,''' buckwheat. Compare ''sarrazin.''—'''Single-grained wheat.''' See ''one-grained wheat,'' above.—'''Spring wheat, summer wheat.''' See def.—'''Tatary wheat,''' the India or Indian wheat, ''Fagopyrum Tataricum.''—'''Tea wheat.''' Same as ''China wheat.''—'''Turkey wheat†, Turkish wheat†,''' Indian corn, vaguely supposed to come from Turkey (compare ''turkey''). Also called ''Guinea wheat'' and ''Indian wheat.''
{{smaller block|There grows in several parts of Africa, Asia, and America a kind of corn called Mays, and such as we commonly name ''Turkey wheat.'' They make bread of it which is hard of digestion, heavy in the stomach, and does not agree with any but such as are of a robust and hail constitution.
{{right|''L. Lemery,'' Treatise on Foods (1704), p. 71. (''Davies.'')}}
We saw a great many fields of Indian corn, which grows to the height of six or seven feet. It is made into flour for the use of the common people, and goes by the name of ''Turkey wheat.''
